Node’s built-in `http` module supports requests over a Unix-domain socket using `socketPath`. Use `socketPath` instead of `host`/`port`:

```js
import http from 'node:http';

const req = http.request(
  {
    socketPath: '/var/run/my-proxy.sock',
    path: '/',
    headers: {
      Host: 'example.com',
    },
  },
  (res) => {
    let body = '';

    res.on('data', (chunk) => {
      body += chunk;
    });

    res.on('end', () => {
      console.log(res.statusCode, body);
    });
  },
);

req.on('error', console.error);
req.end();
```

`socketPath` is the Unix-domain socket path, and it must be used instead of `host`/`port`. The request path defaults to `/` and can include a query string.

The supplied Node.js results do not document a dedicated built-in option for configuring an HTTP proxy whose own endpoint is a Unix-domain socket. They do document `proxyEnv` for proxy configuration through environment variables.
